nswer depends on the graph values shown — state least, greatest and difference correctly from the display","Reading between grid lines carelessly invents values not on the scale","Read 12 from left to right as ones only, ignoring place value"],"question":"Using the method for interpret, which statement is true?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Disagreement is resolved by checking places, operations and the question focus, not by blending answers. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Pick the longer-looking working automatically","Delete units so the answers appear to match","Average the two answers without checking either","Recheck places, operations and whether the question asked for the quantity you computed"],"question":"If two methods disagree on a interpret problem with numbers such as 12 and 7,, what should you check first?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Transfer requires new numbers with the same mathematical method and a named reason. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Retype the worked model numbers 12 and 7, as the answer","Change only handwriting and keep the same numbers","Reading between grid lines carelessly invents values not on the scale","Use the same interpret method on new numbers and name the places or values that matter"],"question":"Solve a fresh interpret problem that uses different numbers from the worked model and explain one key step.?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Recognising the warned misconception protects accuracy on new problems. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Reading graphs means extracting values, comparing categories and stating conclusions that the data actually support","Answer depends on the graph values shown — state least, greatest and difference correctly from the display","Label units when comparing 12 with 7","Reading between grid lines carelessly invents values not on the scale"],"question":"Which error is the lesson warning against for interpret?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept any mathematically valid solution that uses new numbers, shows working for interpret, and names places/values. Reject copied model numbers.","question":"Solve one fresh problem that uses interpret with different numbers from the worked model. Show working and name the places or values that matter.","sample_answer":"Sample: new numbers solved with visible working for interpret; key places/values named. Do not award marks for retyping the model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a named misconception about interpret, corrected working, and a method reason. Reject vague “I was wrong” with no repair.","question":"Describe one likely mistake with interpret, show corrected working, and say why the correction works.","sample_answer":"Sample misconception: Reading between grid lines carelessly invents values not on the scale.. Correction uses Answer depends on the graph values shown — state least, greatest and difference correctly from the display.. Why: restores accurate interpret.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent working and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-mat-y4-w37-l3","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA mathematics alignment using verified outcome code wording where recorded in lesson-pipeline/sources; content-point mapping remains pending.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise interpret.","Read the mission before you start calculating.","Keep the place or unit you are working with in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your working with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the place or distance that carries the method.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore place value or working.","Prefer accurate method over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your working and reason are both present.","Proofread digits, places and operation signs.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact place, value or distance before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key numbers.","Say what each number represents in the method.","A final answer alone is weaker than labelled working."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with stronger working.","Change the value or method, not only how neat it looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose fresh numbers that still use the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original working and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new numbers."]},"subject":"Statistics and Probability","success_criteria":["I can use interpret with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify graph and explain why it matters.","I can practise compare independently on a new prompt.","I can notice conclusion needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Reading graphs means extracting values, comparing categories and stating conclusions that the data actually support.","options":["A calculation unrelated to the numbers given","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no working","Reading graphs means extracting values, comparing categories and stating conclusions that the data actually support"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about interpret with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nIf Sport has 12 and Art has 7, Sport is more popular by 5. A conclusion “everyone loves Sport” overclaims if only one class was surveyed.\n\nTRY FIRST\nFrom a column graph, which category is least and by how many less than the greatest?\n\nFEEDBACK\nAnswer depends on the graph values shown — state least, greatest and difference correctly from the display.","explain":"Reading graphs means extracting values, comparing categories and stating conclusions that the data actually support.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Reading between grid lines carelessly invents values not on the scale. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Interpret graphs fairly.","caption":"Avoid overclaims.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Read value</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">compare</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Conclude</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">only what data show</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which number or place matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the places, values or distances that carry the method before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: If Sport has 12 and Art has 7, Sport is more popular by 5. A conclusion “everyone loves Sport” overclaims if only one cl Then write your own working.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which number or place matters most.\nJuno: Name the places, values or distances that carry the method before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: If Sport has 12 and Art has 7, Sport is more popular by 5. A conclusion “everyone loves Sport” overclaims if only one cl Then write your own working."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid. Supported working follows the actual numbers and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid","A guess with no working","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about interpret with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nIf Sport has 12 and Art has 7, Sport is more popular by 5. A conclusion “everyone loves Sport” overclaims if only one class was surveyed. Look at each number, place or distance that carries the method.\n\nTRY FIRST\nWrite the key numbers from the model and say what each one represents in the method.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful working names the quantities and the reason for each step, not only the final answer.","explain":"Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only the final answer without working does not show the method. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Show the working","visual":[{"alt":"Evidence vs overclaim.","caption":"Four graphs.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Difference</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">greatest−least</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Risk</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">invent mid-scale values</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison.","options":["Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no working"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about interpret with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nReading between grid lines carelessly invents values not on the scale. Here is a more productive model: If Sport has 12 and Art has 7, Sport is more popular by 5. A conclusion “everyone loves Sport” overclaims if only one class was surveyed.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against approach gives the wrong value or meaning, then write corrected working.\n\nFEEDBACK\nAnswer depends on the graph values shown — state least, greatest and difference correctly from the display.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific mathematical error, then show the corrected working.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the answer without repairing the method does not fix the error. Reading between grid lines carelessly invents values not on the scale. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use the method on new numbers or a new context, not a copy of the worked example.","options":["A guess with no working","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given","Use the method on new numbers or a new context, not a copy of the worked example","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about interpret with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nReading graphs means extracting values, comparing categories and stating conclusions that the data actually support. Now choose different numbers to show you can transfer the method.\n\nTRY FIRST\nSolve one fresh problem that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your answer.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new problem should use the same method as the model, but contain different numbers.","explain":"Use the method on new numbers or a new context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy and units or places.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new numbers. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Transfer to a new problem"}],"title":"Year 4, Week 37, Lesson 3: Reading and interpreting graphs","unit_number":10,"unit_title":"Data, chance and Stage 2 review","week":37,"week_in_unit":1,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nIf Sport has 12 and Art has 7, Sport is more popular by 5. A conclusion “everyone loves Sport” overclaims if only one class was surveyed.\n\nWrite what makes this example accurate, naming the places, values or distances used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Interpret graphs fairly.","caption":"Avoid overclaims.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Read value</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">compare</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Conclude</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">only what data show</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Evidence vs overclaim.","caption":"Four graphs.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Difference</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">greatest−least</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Risk</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">invent mid-scale values</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 4"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-mat-y4-w37-l3' AND family_id IS NULL;
